when sabrin announced their rocket  extreme q ssd drives  we instantly pre-ordered one and then a  couple of weeks later  they reached out to us and offered to  send us a couple of these drives  which we obviously accepted so here we  have the two terabyte and four terabyte  models that we've been testing  and so far we've been extremely  impressed because they support very fast  thunderbolt 3 speeds  just as you'd expect from an ssd that's  built into one of apple's modern macs  or a high-end windows machine but  compared to apple's ssds  the severance are better first of all  because  of the price here's a side-by-side chart  showing how much money you save with a  sovereign drive  compared to apple storage upgrade prices  on the 2025k  imac that's a savings of 800. for an 8  terabyte drive  and on top of that it's nearly  impossible to take an apple ssd  off of a mac thanks to them either being  soldered on  or controlled by the t2 chip so if you  get one of these excellent separate  drives  you can take it with you when you  eventually upgrade to an apple silicon  mac  later on in the future and to make it  even better  these severant drives support both on  about 3 and usbc  ports compared to a lot of other  thunderbolt 3 drives  like this one that we custom built which  only works with thunderbolt 3  whereas the severant drives are actually  going to work with basically any modern  computer  like a windows laptop or even an ipad  pro  at a very impressive rated speed of 900  megabytes per second  so the sovereign extreme q drives are  really the optimal choice for  convenience going into the future  especially since it's one of the few  drives available right now  that you can actually get with up to a  massive eight  terabytes of storage now with all of  that said let me finally get into  reviewing the drive  itself starting with the design and  portability the build quality is really  nice with an aluminum finish  all around the ssd and the design is  very subtle and clean  with nothing but two logos on the top  one for sabrin's  and one for thunderbolt on the underside  there are two rubber strips that stop  the aluminum ssd from going all over  your desk  like other drives like this custom one  does and they also help keep the finish  looking great  and the included 3 foot thunderbolt 3  cable matches up to the dark gray  aluminum exterior  very well and for being able to support  up to a massive 8 terabytes of storage  this ssd is incredibly compact while  also having a nice and solid amount of  weight to it this is probably due to the  thermal pad that's included  inside the enclosure for heat  dissipation and because of the slim and  compact design  you can easily slide this into your  pocket or throw it into your laptop bag  to make for the most portable  high-capacity ssd out there now let's  finally get into the technical side of  this storage drive  plugging it in for the first time it  worked exactly as i expected  a blank drive with four terabytes of  capacity  ready to use at any time no driver  installation or reformatting required  and getting into the performance of this  drive we ended up running the same  transfer stress test that we ran  for one of our videos where we compared  the most popular  portable thunderbolt 3 ssds in 2020  running the blackmagic disk speed test  the other one terabyte drives were  faster than this one in terms of read  speed  and the story was actually the same for  the write speed being slower than those  drives  but wait don't judge a drive by its  benchmarks  because this one is actually faster than  the others for large transfers as you'll  see  in just a second the reason for that is  something called slc cache  which basically means that it can write  a certain amount of data really  fast before it slows down and they do  this to make the drives more cost  effective  so looking at the other drives you only  get those high transfer speeds  for a limited amount of data transferred  like only 20 gigs on the g  drive and about 48 gigabytes on the  samsung x5 ssd  so basically if you're transferring a  file from your computer  to the drive that's larger than the  amount of slc cache  then the transfer speed actually slows  down like in the case of the owc drive  slowing all the way down to a minimum  speed of 950 megabytes per second write  speed  after 30 gigs is transferred so we ended  up transferring a large 172 gigabyte  file to each drive  and the differences in slc cache and  minimum speed  caused a huge difference in the amount  of time for the transfer to finish  now this is where saturn's extreme q  drive comes in  both of them even the two terabyte model  finished the test in only one minute and  27 seconds  beating out all of the other drives in  real world transfer speed  even though it was slower in terms of  the write speed  benchmark that's because there was no  slc cache limit for these drives in that  test  running at full speed for the entire 172  gig file transfer  so we ended up transferring a much  larger 403 gig  file and we were surprised to see that  it transferred the entire 403 gigabytes  without hitting an soc cash limit and we  saw the same results  for the 2 terabyte model and as soon as  that transfer finished  i transferred it again and for only  around 20 seconds  the write speed went down to around 1400  to 1500 megabytes per second  before going back up to the full speed  for the rest of the transfer  so that's super impressive and to make  these seven drives even better  they support both thunderbolt 3 and usbc  at the same time and out of those four  drives that we tested  only the c drive came with that same  support  so we transfer that 403 gig file on the  lassie drive and it took  almost twice as long as the seventh  drive because of its soc cache limit  slowing down the write speeds so based  on all of those tests that we ran  these separate drives are actually  faster than those four  drives in terms of real world transfer  speeds  and the cherry on top is that they're  much less  expensive as well the one terabyte model  is only 220 dollars  compared to a huge four hundred and two  dollars  for the la c drive and it's even less  expensive than the owc drive  which we had many issues with and even  though its slc  cash is lower than our two terabyte and  four terabyte models  it still smokes the more expensive  drives  and then moving up to a two terabyte  model the severant is only 360 dollars  compared to 702 for the lessee  and interestingly the two terabyte  sabrand is actually closer to the price  of the one terabyte versions  of the other drives so we would  recommend the two terabyte model  since we didn't hit an soc cache limit  even after 400 gigabytes  and out of all of those drives the  sabrand is much more portable and  compact and it's the only one available  with a massive eight terabytes of  storage for  sixteen hundred dollars which may sound  expensive  but it's eight hundred dollars less than  apple charges  for an eight terabyte ssd that you can't  remove  and you can't take with you so with all  that said let's finish off our testing  with how this  cyber drive compares to the custom ssd  that we built  well that custom drive was actually 23  seconds faster  in our 172 gigabyte transfer test and  that's because the write speeds  were faster than on the sovereign drive  however  there are a few major drawbacks with our  custom-built ssd  that outweigh the faster speeds the main  one is the price  for both the enclosure and the ones here  by ssd  you're looking at 330 dollars for the  custom drive  compared to only 220 for the sovereign  drive  and of course it's already pre-assembled  so you don't have to open it up and  install the drive yourself  but the biggest drawback is the fact  that the custom drive  only supports thunderbolt 3 while the  sabrant  also supports usbc so we were able to  hook it up to our ipad pro and easily  transfer some files  and that level of convenience is why we  recommend it  over the custom ssd so with all of that  said  we can safely say that the new sabrint  extreme q external ssds  are by far the best bang for the bug  drives we've  ever used easily outclassing the most  popular  thunderbolt 3 ssds and even being a  better choice  than our custom built drive and if  you're interested in buying one of these  drives for your mac  your pc or even your ipad then you can  find the links to the various storage  sizes of these new cyber drives  down in the description below and if you  want to see more reviews like this one  go ahead and click the circle above to  subscribe and be sure to check out our  apple product merch like this t-shirt or  one of our masks down below thanks for  watching and we'll see you  in the next video  you

